.TH CCOL_R_MEMPOOL_ALLOC_ENTRY 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_r_mempool_alloc_entry \- allocate an entry from the ranged pool
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cmempool.h>

void *ccol_r_mempool_alloc_entry(ccol_r_mempool *rmp, size_t size);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Allocates memory of the requested size from the best sub-pool. The
function rounds the size up to the nearest power-of-2 pool. If that pool
is empty, the function tries larger pools. The policy
ccol_fallback_at_first_exhaustion is different: under it, that pool
gives the memory from the heap. The fallback behavior depends on the
policy. See ccol_r_mempool_create(3).
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
rmp   Ranged memory pool to allocate from
size  Size in bytes to allocate. It must be > 0 and <= largest_size
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns a pointer to the allocated entry on success
Returns NULL on failure
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
O(1) in the common case, O(number_of_pools) in the worst case
The memory that comes back is uninitialized. Use
ccol_r_mempool_calloc_entry() for zeroed memory
The pointer that comes back is aligned for any object type. That is the
same guarantee that malloc() makes. Any object that you can store in
heap memory can go into a pool entry
The function rounds the size up to the next power-of-2 pool size
The function tries larger and larger pools when the best pool is empty,
unless the policy is ccol_fallback_at_first_exhaustion
Thread-safe if the pool was created with single_threaded=false
The caller must free the entry with ccol_r_mempool_free_entry()
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_r_mempool_calloc_entry (3),
.BR ccol_r_mempool_realloc_entry (3),
.BR ccol_r_mempool_free_entry (3)
